  "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1):\\,{a}_{1} & =3\\cdot {2}^{1-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=3\\cdot {2}^{k-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)\u21d2P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =3\\cdot {2}^{(k+1)-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)\u21d2P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}$ for all $n\\in N.$",
